This feature focuses on performing comprehensive testing, analysis, and configuration of PingOne Risk policies within the PingAccess AUTHGW environment. The goal is to evaluate various risk predictors, understand their behavior and scoring under different scenarios, and determine optimal threshold values for Medium and High risk classifications.

This initiative includes:

Conducting extensive test cases to evaluate how each predictor behaves.

Capturing test results and logs from Splunk and PingOne audit logs.

Analyzing real-world and simulated scenarios using tools like WonderProxy and device emulators.

Evaluating the impact of predictors.

Fine-tuning the Risk Policy configuration in PingOne by setting appropriate scores for Medium and High per predictor.

Implementing the finalized risk policy in Test, Acceptance, and Production environments for all PingAccess AUTHGW web applications in monitoring mode initially (no enforcement).

Documenting all test results, configurations, and recommendations.

Acceptance Criteria:
✅ All relevant PingOne risk predictors are evaluated with various scenarios (internal/external IPs, new/known devices, proxy networks, location changes, velocity thresholds, etc.).

✅ Detailed test results and observations documentation are created (organized by predictor, score outcome, and reasoning).

✅ Defined and agreed thresholds for Medium and High risk for each predictor, based on data analysis.

✅ A finalized Risk Policy configuration is defined and validated in one lower environment.

✅ The same Risk Policy is rolled out in Test, ACPT, and PROD environments for all AUTHGW web applications, initially in monitoring mode.

✅ Optional: Report or dashboard updates are made in Splunk to track ongoing risk scores and patterns.

✅ All documentation is shared with stakeholders and stored in Confluence or a designated collaboration space.

Benefits:

Helps improve app security by detecting risky behavior.

Gives better visibility into login behavior using real test data.

Avoids false positives and unnecessary MFA prompts.

Prepares the policy for future enforcement when needed.

Enables PingRisk across all AUTHGW apps in a clean and tested way.
